00:23:49,625 --> 00:23:51,082
<i>Finish prepping those samples,</i>

252
00:23:51,083 --> 00:23:54,207
<i>and hopefully we can sequence
the infestation's DNA.</i>

253
00:23:54,208 --> 00:23:55,291
Gotcha.

254
00:23:56,666 --> 00:23:58,457
[sighs, mutters]

255
00:23:58,458 --> 00:24:01,041
[Thorn breathing shakily]

256
00:24:09,875 --> 00:24:11,040
[groaning]

257
00:24:11,041 --> 00:24:13,750
[speaking foreign language]

258
00:24:20,125 --> 00:24:22,250
[Lark speaking foreign language]

259
00:24:27,250 --> 00:24:28,624
[speaking foreign language]

260
00:24:28,625 --> 00:24:31,458
[breathing shakily, groaning]

261
00:24:33,291 --> 00:24:34,625
[speaking foreign language]

262
00:24:46,250 --> 00:24:48,082
[shuddering, wheezing]

263
00:24:48,083 --> 00:24:49,416
[shushes]

264
00:24:50,583 --> 00:24:52,374
[speaking foreign language]

265
00:24:52,375 --> 00:24:54,583
[Thorn wheezing]

266
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,041
[Lark gasps, cries]

267
00:25:00,041 --> 00:25:05,208
[speaking foreign language]

268
00:25:07,625 --> 00:25:09,958
- [shouting, grunting]
- [Hera shushes]

269
00:25:11,041 --> 00:25:13,500
[Hera, Lark speaking foreign language]

270
00:25:19,916 --> 00:25:20,915
[Hera sighs]

271
00:25:20,916 --> 00:25:22,166
[speaking foreign language]

272
00:25:25,041 --> 00:25:26,041
[sighs]

273
00:25:27,416 --> 00:25:30,375
- [speaking foreign language]
- [Thorn breathing shakily]

274
00:25:37,750 --> 00:25:39,750
[Lark humming]

275
00:25:42,166 --> 00:25:45,125
[Thorn breathes deeply]

276
00:25:50,208 --> 00:25:51,583
[device beeping]

277
00:25:52,625 --> 00:25:56,291
[humming continues]

278
00:26:21,666 --> 00:26:22,666
[line rings]

279
00:26:24,041 --> 00:26:25,915
[Greg on laptop] <i>What? Hey. Whoa.</i>

280
00:26:25,916 --> 00:26:28,207
- [Claire] Oh, wait, were you sleeping?
- <i>Hi.</i>

281
00:26:28,208 --> 00:26:29,582
<i>Nope, I was not.</i>

282
00:26:29,583 --> 00:26:33,124
<i>I was-- I was, like, laying down.
I was resting my eyes, but I'm not asleep.</i>

283
00:26:33,125 --> 00:26:35,249
Oh, no, I forgot how late it is there.

284
00:26:35,250 --> 00:26:36,665
<i>It is not. No, it's-- I'm wide awake.</i>

285
00:26:36,666 --> 00:26:38,207
- You're lying.
- [chuckles] <i>I'm not lying.</i>

286
00:26:38,208 --> 00:26:39,874
<i>Look, I'm wide awake.</i>

287
00:26:39,875 --> 00:26:42,707
<i>Wide awake. Ready to talk. Boom.</i>

288
00:26:42,708 --> 00:26:44,374
[chuckles] Hello.

289
00:26:44,375 --> 00:26:45,833
- <i>Hi.</i>
- Hi.

290
00:26:49,291 --> 00:26:52,791
- Do you wanna see my yearbook?
- <i>Uh, desperately.</i> [chuckles]

291
00:26:54,500 --> 00:26:55,791
Okay.

292
00:26:57,791 --> 00:26:59,416
[strains] All right. [sighs]

293
00:27:01,583 --> 00:27:02,832
Okay, let's see.

294
00:27:02,833 --> 00:27:05,375
Oh, debate.

295
00:27:06,416 --> 00:27:07,582
- <i>Wow.</i>
- Mm-hmm.

296
00:27:07,583 --> 00:27:09,999
<i>Wait, how old are you here?</i>

297
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:11,374
Sophomore year.

298
00:27:11,375 --> 00:27:13,000
<i>Yeah, I bet you were great at debate.</i>

299
00:27:13,583 --> 00:27:14,540
I was good.

300
00:27:14,541 --> 00:27:16,624
- Yeah, I made a girl cry.
- <i>Yeah.</i>

301
00:27:16,625 --> 00:27:20,457
[laughs] <i>Yeah, of course.</i>

302
00:27:20,458 --> 00:27:21,708
<i>Of course you did.</i>

303
00:27:24,083 --> 00:27:25,333
This is weird.

304
00:27:26,208 --> 00:27:27,208
<i>What?</i>

305
00:27:27,791 --> 00:27:30,875
I never had a boy on my bed here before.

306
00:27:31,875 --> 00:27:36,665
<i>Oh. Oh, well, um--
well, it's an honor to be here.</i>

307
00:27:36,666 --> 00:27:38,374
[chuckles]

308
00:27:38,375 --> 00:27:40,040
[Greg speaks indistinctly]

309
00:27:40,041 --> 00:27:41,166
Hello?

310
00:27:41,708 --> 00:27:44,957
Wait, I think
you're breaking up a little bit.

311
00:27:44,958 --> 00:27:46,124
[Greg speaks indistinctly]

312
00:27:46,125 --> 00:27:47,333
Hello?

313
00:27:47,833 --> 00:27:49,082
[no audible dialogue]

314
00:27:49,083 --> 00:27:50,999
- [speaks indistinctly] <i>Oh, hey.</i>
- Can you hear me?

315
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:52,790
- <i>Yes. Wait, can you hear me?</i>
- Now?

316
00:27:52,791 --> 00:27:54,165
Yep, yep, yep.

317
00:27:54,166 --> 00:27:56,457
Gotta get down here. [chuckles]

318
00:27:56,458 --> 00:27:58,166
[laughing] <i>Hey.</i>

319
00:28:01,083 --> 00:28:02,083
Hi.

320
00:28:03,708 --> 00:28:04,708
<i>Hi.</i>

321
00:28:10,416 --> 00:28:11,916
My mom's dying.

322
00:28:14,250 --> 00:28:15,958
Doing what they can, but, um,

323
00:28:17,875 --> 00:28:19,000
it's not looking good.

324
00:28:20,666 --> 00:28:25,833
<i>Oh. Oh, Cl-- Uh, Claire, I-- Um...</i>

325
00:28:27,750 --> 00:28:29,291
<i>God, I'm sorry.</i>

326
00:28:33,458 --> 00:28:34,458
Me too.

327
00:28:39,166 --> 00:28:40,166
[sniffles]

328
00:28:45,250 --> 00:28:46,583
[sighs]

329
00:28:49,750 --> 00:28:52,791
[chuckles] <i>You're sideways.</i>

330
00:28:58,541 --> 00:28:59,875
So are you.

331
00:29:02,583 --> 00:29:04,833
[sighs]

332
00:29:07,625 --> 00:29:09,916
[Thorn breathing heavily]

333
00:29:34,208 --> 00:29:38,166
[Coakley] It must be nice not to, like,
waste time worrying about dying.

334
00:29:38,750 --> 00:29:42,165
[ROSCO] <i>I think about death sometimes.
I wouldn't say I worry though.</i>

335
00:29:42,166 --> 00:29:44,790
- Hmm.
- <i>Do you think about death?</i>

336
00:29:44,791 --> 00:29:46,375
Ordinarily, no.

337
00:29:47,791 --> 00:29:49,583
<i>What's it like when you think?</i>

338
00:29:51,250 --> 00:29:52,541
I don't know.

339
00:29:54,041 --> 00:29:55,708
My neurons are firing.

340
00:29:56,791 --> 00:29:59,874
<i>I understand it
from a mechanical standpoint.</i>

341
00:29:59,875 --> 00:30:00,874
Uh-huh.

342
00:30:00,875 --> 00:30:04,125
<i>But I'm curious to hear...</i>

343
00:30:04,708 --> 00:30:06,458
<i>What is it like?</i>

344
00:30:08,708 --> 00:30:10,249
ROSCO, I-I don't know.

345
00:30:10,250 --> 00:30:14,707
Um, can you turn off
all the nonessential electrical units

346
00:30:14,708 --> 00:30:16,457
so we can save on O2 waste?

347
00:30:16,458 --> 00:30:18,624
<i>It won't make much of a difference.</i>

348
00:30:18,625 --> 00:30:19,790
A little though?

349
00:30:19,791 --> 00:30:20,999
<i>Of course. A little.</i>

350
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:22,083
All right.

351
00:30:24,208 --> 00:30:26,041
Turn 'em all off. I'm fine in the dark.

352
00:30:29,208 --> 00:30:30,750
Okay. [sighs]

353
00:30:35,208 --> 00:30:38,000
Sorry. I'm sorry.
What's it like when you think?

354
00:30:38,666 --> 00:30:44,290
<i>I think that you humans feel,
at any given moment, like you--</i>

355
00:30:44,291 --> 00:30:46,958
<i>your mind is in one place.</i>

356
00:30:47,458 --> 00:30:52,125
<i>I have many more moments,
many more places. Does that make sense?</i>

357
00:30:52,916 --> 00:30:54,375
- Yeah.
- [chimes]

358
00:30:55,625 --> 00:30:56,832
What was that?

359
00:30:56,833 --> 00:30:59,833
<i>I have a formula
for an antiviral that may work.</i>

360
00:31:04,500 --> 00:31:07,540
[Thorn breathing deeply]

361
00:31:07,541 --> 00:31:09,583
[waves crashing]

362
00:31:13,041 --> 00:31:14,333
[stones clacking]

363
00:31:15,458 --> 00:31:18,041
[speaking foreign language]

364
00:31:21,833 --> 00:31:24,750
[speaking foreign language]

365
00:31:28,083 --> 00:31:31,000
[speaking foreign language]

366
00:31:32,291 --> 00:31:34,291
[stones clacking]

367
00:31:37,500 --> 00:31:38,666
[Lark gasps]

368
00:31:42,250 --> 00:31:45,583
[speaking foreign language]

369
00:32:02,541 --> 00:32:04,583
[Lark screams]

370
00:32:10,833 --> 00:32:12,791
[Hera sobbing]

371
00:32:14,166 --> 00:32:16,125
[whimpering]

372
00:32:39,375 --> 00:32:42,250
[sobbing]

373
00:32:57,541 --> 00:32:59,541
[whimpering continues]

374
00:33:13,875 --> 00:33:16,041
[sobbing]

375
00:33:54,291 --> 00:33:55,416
[speaking foreign language]

376
00:34:07,500 --> 00:34:08,750
[speaking foreign language]

377
00:34:54,916 --> 00:34:59,583
I have removed an object
which appears to be organic material,

378
00:35:00,083 --> 00:35:02,916
but until we do an MRI,
we can't be positive...

379
00:35:03,750 --> 00:35:07,083
- [phone chimes]
- ...exactly what we're looking at.

380
00:35:13,333 --> 00:35:17,207
No, Mom. No, don't say that, okay?

381
00:35:17,208 --> 00:35:20,416
We're-- We're just gonna deal with this.
No, no, no, no. [shushes] Mom,

382
00:35:21,041 --> 00:35:23,124
<i>what if I, um, maybe-- maybe--</i>

383
00:35:23,125 --> 00:35:25,499
<i>Okay...</i> [stammers] <i>...I'll come out Friday.</i>

384
00:35:25,500 --> 00:35:28,625
<i>Okay? Because I want to. That's why.</i>

385
00:35:34,625 --> 00:35:40,833
I, uh-- I'm-- I'm gonna have to
leave my fellowship.

386
00:35:42,500 --> 00:35:45,083
I gotta take care of my mom, and, um,

387
00:35:46,958 --> 00:35:50,416
I have a meeting with the dean in,
like, an hour.

388
00:35:52,583 --> 00:35:53,583
Oh.

389
00:35:55,208 --> 00:35:56,208
You know this.

390
00:35:56,791 --> 00:35:59,583
Uh, about your mom, yeah, but n-not, uh...

391
00:36:00,083 --> 00:36:04,249
Yeah. So bye-bye
career-changing work or whatever.

392
00:36:04,250 --> 00:36:06,958
[breathes deeply]

393
00:36:09,500 --> 00:36:10,833
I have to shower.

394
00:36:13,416 --> 00:36:15,833
- Yeah. No, yes, go shower.
- Okay.

395
00:36:20,666 --> 00:36:22,666
[shower running]

396
00:36:23,541 --> 00:36:24,916
[sighs]

397
00:36:40,916 --> 00:36:41,916
So...

398
00:36:43,833 --> 00:36:45,249
we're still losing palms.

399
00:36:45,250 --> 00:36:46,333
[ROSCO] <i>Yes.</i>

400
00:36:48,125 --> 00:36:49,999
The antivirals aren't working.

401
00:36:50,000 --> 00:36:51,083
<i>No.</i>

402
00:36:58,000 --> 00:36:59,375
I honestly don't...

403
00:37:04,916 --> 00:37:05,916
<i>What?</i>

404
00:37:10,416 --> 00:37:11,458
[machine chimes]

405
00:37:18,083 --> 00:37:19,875
[Coakley] <i>What if we cut our losses here?</i>

406
00:37:22,125 --> 00:37:24,250
ROSCO, humor me.

407
00:37:25,208 --> 00:37:26,416
[ROSCO] <i>What do you mean?</i>

408
00:37:27,291 --> 00:37:28,999
We can't save these palms.

409
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:31,124
<i>The palms create the oxygen needed
for life on board--</i>

410
00:37:31,125 --> 00:37:34,374
I know, I know. [stammers] Yeah,
but these ones are gonna die

411
00:37:34,375 --> 00:37:37,124
because they have an unforeseen parasite
that we can't kill.

412
00:37:37,125 --> 00:37:41,291
<i>Yet. We may still be able to create
a strain of palm that is immune.</i>

413
00:37:42,833 --> 00:37:45,915
But the one thing we know will kill
the virus is if we kill the host.

414
00:37:45,916 --> 00:37:47,832
- Yeah?
- <i>Yes.</i>

415
00:37:47,833 --> 00:37:51,000
So, why don't we kill the host?

416
00:37:51,916 --> 00:37:53,166
<i>I don't follow.</i>

417
00:37:54,500 --> 00:37:56,041
We kill the palms.

418
00:37:56,625 --> 00:38:00,540
We get rid of all the palms,
and we jettison the soil,

419
00:38:00,541 --> 00:38:02,707
and we wait, uh, a day, a week--
I don't know.

420
00:38:02,708 --> 00:38:05,665
How long can we survive
without fresh oxygen?

421
00:38:05,666 --> 00:38:07,375
<i>That depends on your heart rate.</i>

422
00:38:08,250 --> 00:38:10,124
Okay, but you see what I'm saying, right?

423
00:38:10,125 --> 00:38:11,832
Why are we trying to salvage these plants?

424
00:38:11,833 --> 00:38:15,125
We could just kill them and kill the virus
and then plant new ones.

425
00:38:15,625 --> 00:38:17,332
<i>If we kill all the palms,</i>

426
00:38:17,333 --> 00:38:21,374
<i>there won't be enough oxygen for two years
while the new ones grow in.</i>

427
00:38:21,375 --> 00:38:25,624
<i>Plus it would take the entire journey
to reach proper O2 levels for the babies.</i>

428
00:38:25,625 --> 00:38:28,290
[sighs] Okay.

429
00:38:28,291 --> 00:38:32,665
Um, what are the top five uses of oxygen
on the ship?

430
00:38:32,666 --> 00:38:34,790
<i>Life support for you and the embryos.</i>

431
00:38:34,791 --> 00:38:36,165
What else?

432
00:38:36,166 --> 00:38:38,999
<i>The other uses are negligible
by comparison.</i>

433
00:38:39,000 --> 00:38:40,915
<i>You're the organic systems.</i>

434
00:38:40,916 --> 00:38:43,250
<i>You use 98% of the oxygen.</i>

435
00:38:50,333 --> 00:38:54,124
Well, then the organic systems
aren't going to make it.

436
00:38:54,125 --> 00:38:57,791
<i>But both you and the babies
are mission critical.</i>

437
00:39:00,458 --> 00:39:01,458
Well, what if--

438
00:39:02,500 --> 00:39:03,750
what if we eliminate one?

439
00:39:04,625 --> 00:39:05,625
<i>Which one?</i>

440
00:39:09,208 --> 00:39:11,083
I use the most oxygen.

441
00:39:12,250 --> 00:39:16,040
So what if I kill myself,

442
00:39:16,041 --> 00:39:19,874
and then you and the ship continue on?

443
00:39:19,875 --> 00:39:24,290
And when you get to Kepler,
most of the labor can be automated.

444
00:39:24,291 --> 00:39:28,790
So you would manage the drones
and incubate the stem cells

445
00:39:28,791 --> 00:39:31,125
and raise the kids
and pass the whole thing off.

446
00:39:32,500 --> 00:39:33,500
Would that work?

447
00:39:34,166 --> 00:39:37,125
<i>Uh, I'm not equipped for...</i>

448
00:39:37,625 --> 00:39:38,625
<i>No.</i>

449
00:39:40,000 --> 00:39:41,000
Are you sure?

450
00:39:42,333 --> 00:39:44,665
[sighs] <i>I'd need to run through
every protocol</i>

451
00:39:44,666 --> 00:39:46,583
<i>for the rest of the mission, but...</i>

452
00:39:49,916 --> 00:39:50,916
Conversely...

453
00:39:52,333 --> 00:39:55,832
<i>We find a way to generate
additional oxygen?</i>

454
00:39:55,833 --> 00:39:57,750
<i>A backup greenhouse?</i>

455
00:40:02,208 --> 00:40:03,374
Would that work?

456
00:40:03,375 --> 00:40:04,832
<i>I don't know.</i>

457
00:40:04,833 --> 00:40:08,040
<i>We can't make space for it
without losing an essential system,</i>

458
00:40:08,041 --> 00:40:10,875
<i>but I could run calculations to be sure.</i>

459
00:40:11,458 --> 00:40:12,458
Okay.

460
00:40:17,750 --> 00:40:18,791
[sighs]

461
00:40:20,958 --> 00:40:23,541
[no audible dialogue]

462
00:40:42,083 --> 00:40:45,916
[Claire sobbing]

463
00:41:19,791 --> 00:41:21,916
[crickets chirping]

464
00:41:32,000 --> 00:41:38,124
[ROSCO] <i>I was thinking, I figured out
where to put a secondary greenhouse.</i>

465
00:41:38,125 --> 00:41:39,208
Mm-hmm.

466
00:41:40,250 --> 00:41:42,041
<i>In the computer space.</i>

467
00:41:42,708 --> 00:41:47,083
<i>Convert my mainframe to plants,
use the algae on board.</i>

468
00:41:51,875 --> 00:41:54,249
- But...
- <i>It's the perfect spot.</i>

469
00:41:54,250 --> 00:41:57,582
<i>It'll feed oxygen directly
to the life-support system.</i>

470
00:41:57,583 --> 00:42:00,165
Yeah, but you'd be fried, like, permanently.

471
00:42:00,166 --> 00:42:01,250
<i>Yes.</i>

472
00:42:03,833 --> 00:42:04,875
Doesn't that bother you?

473
00:42:05,375 --> 00:42:06,375
<i>No.</i>

474
00:42:09,208 --> 00:42:10,624
<i>The best-use case for me is</i>

475
00:42:10,625 --> 00:42:13,166
<i>that you survive
and make it to the planet.</i>

476
00:42:13,875 --> 00:42:15,875
<i>That would be my recommendation.</i>

477
00:42:19,041 --> 00:42:22,000
No, no, I'm not choosing
between you or me.

478
00:42:23,500 --> 00:42:28,083
<i>Let me run a simulation and see
who's more likely to complete the mission.</i>

479
00:42:32,833 --> 00:42:34,875
[inhales deeply]

480
00:42:39,333 --> 00:42:40,333
Okay.

481
00:42:53,916 --> 00:42:55,875
[grunts, pants]

482
00:43:06,208 --> 00:43:08,333
[groaning]

483
00:43:09,166 --> 00:43:10,290
[speaking foreign language]

484
00:43:10,291 --> 00:43:12,415
[breathing sharply]

485
00:43:12,416 --> 00:43:13,916
[grunts]

486
00:43:18,041 --> 00:43:19,040
[Lark breathing sharply]

487
00:43:19,041 --> 00:43:20,165
[speaking foreign language]

488
00:43:20,166 --> 00:43:22,416
[Hera breathing heavily]

489
00:43:24,291 --> 00:43:25,791
[grunting]

490
00:43:29,041 --> 00:43:30,541
[grunting continues]

491
00:43:33,791 --> 00:43:38,000
[speaking foreign language]

492
00:43:47,458 --> 00:43:50,166
