- [groans]

761
01:08:15,083 --> 01:08:17,083
- It's the future.
- Okay.

762
01:08:18,333 --> 01:08:19,333
Okay.

763
01:08:20,333 --> 01:08:21,582
I think we should move.

764
01:08:21,583 --> 01:08:23,874
I-- What?
What are we talking about right now?

765
01:08:23,875 --> 01:08:28,208
Like, I think we should
move out of this house.

766
01:08:30,458 --> 01:08:32,666
It just feels like the past, you know?

767
01:08:33,166 --> 01:08:34,791
Oh, I like this house.

768
01:08:35,291 --> 01:08:37,541
I-I just don't want our kids
to feel stuck.

769
01:08:38,875 --> 01:08:43,290
Well, they are four and zero.

770
01:08:43,291 --> 01:08:46,082
So they're gonna feel stuck
wherever we put them.

771
01:08:46,083 --> 01:08:48,290
W-Why are you so neurotic all of a sudden?

772
01:08:48,291 --> 01:08:50,082
Should I submit for this professorship?

773
01:08:50,083 --> 01:08:51,750
Is that what this is about?

774
01:08:52,250 --> 01:08:53,665
I already wrote the application.

775
01:08:53,666 --> 01:08:54,750
You did?

776
01:08:55,708 --> 01:08:58,540
Should I? I shouldn't.

777
01:08:58,541 --> 01:09:01,041
- Oh, I should?
- [stammers] We should go to bed.

778
01:09:02,791 --> 01:09:03,791
Yeah.

779
01:09:25,916 --> 01:09:27,458
Position's full-time?

780
01:09:29,625 --> 01:09:31,208
Yeah. [sighs]

781
01:09:32,458 --> 01:09:36,540
[Greg] I guess the question is,
if you're there, will you miss being here?

782
01:09:36,541 --> 01:09:38,958
And if you're here,
will you miss being there?

783
01:09:41,041 --> 01:09:42,041
[sighs]

784
01:09:43,041 --> 01:09:44,041
Exactly.

785
01:09:54,500 --> 01:09:56,541
[dog barking]

786
01:10:07,166 --> 01:10:09,125
[villagers chattering]

787
01:10:16,166 --> 01:10:17,708
[chattering continues]

788
01:10:29,375 --> 01:10:32,790
[Coakley] Our old home was dying,
so we had to leave.

789
01:10:32,791 --> 01:10:35,082
And now we're making a new home.

790
01:10:35,083 --> 01:10:37,583
And this one we're gonna take
really good care of.

791
01:10:38,125 --> 01:10:41,666
And those are all the drones
that are going around the new planet.

792
01:10:42,333 --> 01:10:44,708
And that is drone seven.

793
01:10:45,208 --> 01:10:46,582
There are 50 of them.

794
01:10:46,583 --> 01:10:48,875
They're flying all around.
They're planting trees.

795
01:10:49,375 --> 01:10:52,374
They're-- They're shooting seeds
in the ground with a little seed gun.

796
01:10:52,375 --> 01:10:54,375
Isn't that cool? Yeah.

797
01:10:55,041 --> 01:10:59,790
There's gonna be enough air down there
in 12 years and 10 months.

798
01:10:59,791 --> 01:11:01,958
- [child] Then we will live there?
- Yeah.

799
01:11:02,458 --> 01:11:05,666
When you're older, like me.

800
01:11:06,708 --> 01:11:07,957
Will you be older?

801
01:11:07,958 --> 01:11:09,915
[inhales sharply] Uh, no.

802
01:11:09,916 --> 01:11:12,458
You'll get older,
but I'll-- I'll be the same.

803
01:11:13,500 --> 01:11:14,500
Why?

804
01:11:15,500 --> 01:11:17,333
Um, it's how we were made.

805
01:11:18,000 --> 01:11:19,666
We're-- We're not the same.

806
01:11:20,708 --> 01:11:21,916
We are different?

807
01:11:23,166 --> 01:11:27,999
Yes. Yes, because you, um, you grow up,
like, really, really fast,

808
01:11:28,000 --> 01:11:32,333
and I grow really, really slow. [chuckles]

809
01:11:33,916 --> 01:11:36,958
Why won't we both grow the same?

810
01:11:37,666 --> 01:11:40,790
Um, well...

811
01:11:40,791 --> 01:11:44,749
[sighs] ...a long time ago people decided

812
01:11:44,750 --> 01:11:47,999
that it was actually bad
to live a long time.

813
01:11:48,000 --> 01:11:49,416
So it's bad for you?

814
01:11:49,916 --> 01:11:51,624
[stammers] It's not bad. It's just--

815
01:11:51,625 --> 01:11:53,583
[stammers] Only for certain people.

816
01:11:54,416 --> 01:11:56,040
People who have to live a long time.

817
01:11:56,041 --> 01:11:57,374
And I don't have to?

818
01:11:57,375 --> 01:11:59,125
No, you don't have to.

819
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:02,582
I do 'cause I have to keep an eye on you.

820
01:12:02,583 --> 01:12:03,666
[chuckles]

821
01:12:04,833 --> 01:12:06,750
Then I will be older than you?

822
01:12:09,041 --> 01:12:11,125
Yeah. Someday, yeah.

823
01:12:13,000 --> 01:12:15,833
[children clamoring, laughing]

824
01:12:24,375 --> 01:12:25,999
[speaking foreign language]

825
01:12:26,000 --> 01:12:27,124
[Thorn grunts]

826
01:12:27,125 --> 01:12:28,791
[speaking foreign language]

827
01:12:31,916 --> 01:12:33,415
[children chuckling]

828
01:12:33,416 --> 01:12:35,707
[speaking foreign language]

829
01:12:35,708 --> 01:12:37,832
[shouts in foreign language]

830
01:12:37,833 --> 01:12:39,582
[children scream, laugh]

831
01:12:39,583 --> 01:12:40,791
[Thorn laughs]

832
01:13:16,583 --> 01:13:17,707
[Greg] You recognize these guys?

833
01:13:17,708 --> 01:13:21,749
This is one of our earliest ancestors,
the Neanderthals.

834
01:13:21,750 --> 01:13:23,457
Can you tell me something
about Neanderthals?

835
01:13:23,458 --> 01:13:24,540
These are the cavemen?

836
01:13:24,541 --> 01:13:26,915
Nailed it. Nailed it first try.

837
01:13:26,916 --> 01:13:30,790
Did your mom ever tell you that you are
one of our favorite students? I think.

838
01:13:30,791 --> 01:13:33,249
- The only student. Hi.
- [Greg] Oh, yeah. I keep forgetting.

839
01:13:33,250 --> 01:13:34,665
[chuckles]

840
01:13:34,666 --> 01:13:37,707
- Did you already start without me?
- [Greg] We did. Just a little bit.

841
01:13:37,708 --> 01:13:38,790
[Claire, Greg kiss]

842
01:13:38,791 --> 01:13:41,250
Does anyone know
what happened to Neanderthals?

843
01:13:42,291 --> 01:13:44,332
- [student 1] Dinosaurs?
- [docent chuckles] No.

844
01:13:44,333 --> 01:13:46,000
We did. Other people.

845
01:13:46,500 --> 01:13:48,415
Does anyone know what species we are?

846
01:13:48,416 --> 01:13:49,665
[student 2] Homo sapiens?

847
01:13:49,666 --> 01:13:53,082
That's right.
Homo sapiens replaced Neanderthals.

848
01:13:53,083 --> 01:13:54,290
We were smarter.

849
01:13:54,291 --> 01:13:55,666
How did you know that?

850
01:13:56,250 --> 01:13:58,207
- [docent] Uh, know what?
- That we were smarter.

851
01:13:58,208 --> 01:14:02,540
Uh, well, we made tools.
We created sophisticated art.

852
01:14:02,541 --> 01:14:04,333
And that flute isn't art?

853
01:14:05,250 --> 01:14:06,457
It's a misnomer.

854
01:14:06,458 --> 01:14:08,916
Neanderthals
were actually very sophisticated.

855
01:14:09,708 --> 01:14:11,249
Oh, okay.

856
01:14:11,250 --> 01:14:12,333
Thank you.

857
01:14:13,375 --> 01:14:15,833
Let's move on down
to see the saber-toothed tiger.

858
01:14:18,833 --> 01:14:22,874
Okay. We have to remember
that we don't interrupt other classes.

859
01:14:22,875 --> 01:14:23,957
Sorry.

860
01:14:23,958 --> 01:14:25,582
No, it was a really good question.

861
01:14:25,583 --> 01:14:28,915
Homo sapiens and Neanderthals coexisted
for millennia.

862
01:14:28,916 --> 01:14:30,457
What does that mean?

863
01:14:30,458 --> 01:14:31,540
They lived together.

864
01:14:31,541 --> 01:14:32,916
They made families.

865
01:15:12,208 --> 01:15:16,291
[elder speaking foreign language]

866
01:15:20,833 --> 01:15:22,833
[villagers cheering]

867
01:15:28,750 --> 01:15:30,832
I don't like the babies.

868
01:15:30,833 --> 01:15:32,749
You know you used to be a baby, right?

869
01:15:32,750 --> 01:15:33,833
No.

870
01:15:34,500 --> 01:15:36,207
[chuckling] Okay. Great.

871
01:15:36,208 --> 01:15:37,999
Let's do our chores. Come on.

872
01:15:38,000 --> 01:15:39,124
Now?

873
01:15:39,125 --> 01:15:40,957
Yes, now.

874
01:15:40,958 --> 01:15:42,750
You know what we say,

875
01:15:43,333 --> 01:15:44,457
we only get there...

876
01:15:44,458 --> 01:15:47,458
If everyone does their part.

877
01:15:48,458 --> 01:15:49,458
[Coakley] Good job.

878
01:15:50,500 --> 01:15:51,500
Okay.

879
01:15:54,125 --> 01:15:55,291
There you go.

880
01:15:57,958 --> 01:16:00,708
Thank you, ROSCO.

881
01:16:21,708 --> 01:16:22,916
[exclaiming]

882
01:16:24,625 --> 01:16:27,208
[villagers cheering]

883
01:16:28,791 --> 01:16:29,790
[Claire] Uh, Greg.

884
01:16:29,791 --> 01:16:31,124
- [moaning on laptop]
- [Greg] What?

885
01:16:31,125 --> 01:16:32,708
[Claire] Can you come up here, please?

886
01:16:34,000 --> 01:16:36,290
[Greg clears throat]

887
01:16:36,291 --> 01:16:38,749
- Yeah, what is it?
- New parenting challenge for you.

888
01:16:38,750 --> 01:16:39,833
Pass.

889
01:16:40,791 --> 01:16:43,333
[chuckles] Oh, wow.

890
01:16:44,166 --> 01:16:45,249
Our son's jerking it.

891
01:16:45,250 --> 01:16:46,415
[chuckling] Yeah.

892
01:16:46,416 --> 01:16:47,957
[sighs]

893
01:16:47,958 --> 01:16:49,415
Why were you looking at his, uh--

894
01:16:49,416 --> 01:16:51,332
I-I wasn't snooping, I swear.

895
01:16:51,333 --> 01:16:53,791
I was up here to print that paper.

896
01:16:54,708 --> 01:16:57,290
So, do you want this to be
a father-son moment,

897
01:16:57,291 --> 01:16:58,665
or do you want me to talk to him?

898
01:16:58,666 --> 01:17:01,707
Uh, I don't-- Is there really
anything to say? 'Cause--

899
01:17:01,708 --> 01:17:04,915
About human sexuality?
The ethics of porn consumption?

900
01:17:04,916 --> 01:17:07,415
Okay. Then yes, I will talk to him.

901
01:17:07,416 --> 01:17:09,458
- Just gonna google it.
- [sighs]

902
01:17:10,333 --> 01:17:13,374
"What to say
when you catch your son jerking it."

903
01:17:13,375 --> 01:17:16,499
[chuckling] Well, I'm sorry,
we can't all be gifted teachers like you.

904
01:17:16,500 --> 01:17:17,582
Oh, my God.

905
01:17:17,583 --> 01:17:20,290
The years of homeschooling wasted.

906
01:17:20,291 --> 01:17:22,457
I guess that's just the nature of life, right?

907
01:17:22,458 --> 01:17:26,165
You just-- You start out like this cute,
pure little thing,

908
01:17:26,166 --> 01:17:29,874
and then you just develop
into a damn freak.

909
01:17:29,875 --> 01:17:32,249
[chuckles] Takes after his mother.

910
01:17:32,250 --> 01:17:35,041
Uh, should we look at his browser history?

911
01:17:37,166 --> 01:17:39,040
Okay. Well, yes, porn.

912
01:17:39,041 --> 01:17:42,207
But also science.
Look, so much science. Wow.

913
01:17:42,208 --> 01:17:45,041
He was reading about autophagy on his own?

914
01:17:45,541 --> 01:17:47,582
"Human genome studies."

915
01:17:47,583 --> 01:17:51,374
All right. Well, I think he seems pretty
well-balanced, all things considered.

916
01:17:51,375 --> 01:17:52,457
[both chuckle]

917
01:17:52,458 --> 01:17:54,165
[sighs]

918
01:17:54,166 --> 01:17:55,333
- Okay.
- [machine beeping]

919
01:17:57,041 --> 01:17:58,041
[grunts]

920
01:17:58,541 --> 01:17:59,749
I can't do it.

921
01:17:59,750 --> 01:18:02,040
All right.
I can see you're getting cranky.

922
01:18:02,041 --> 01:18:04,290
This is stupid.
I hate doing the simulations.

923
01:18:04,291 --> 01:18:06,124
- And--
- Hey, listen to me. Listen to me.

924
01:18:06,125 --> 01:18:08,457
If something happens to me
and I can't land this ship,

925
01:18:08,458 --> 01:18:09,874
you are second-in-command.

926
01:18:09,875 --> 01:18:11,083
You are in charge.

927
01:18:12,375 --> 01:18:15,082
So you can be mad about it now,
but you don't have a choice.

928
01:18:15,083 --> 01:18:16,375
You gotta learn this.

929
01:18:17,000 --> 01:18:18,000
No running!

930
01:18:23,125 --> 01:18:25,750
'Cause if you can't do it right,
then no one survives.

931
01:18:26,875 --> 01:18:27,916
You understand?

932
01:18:38,000 --> 01:18:39,791
[speaker] Imagine we could stop time.

933
01:18:43,583 --> 01:18:48,041
{\an8}We could press pause on something that's
plagued mankind from the very beginning

934
01:18:49,333 --> 01:18:50,958
{\an8}and we could, just maybe...

935
01:18:53,291 --> 01:18:54,707
{\an8}live forever.

936
01:18:54,708 --> 01:18:56,582
[audience clamoring]

937
01:18:56,583 --> 01:18:57,666
It's a pipe dream.

938
01:18:58,791 --> 01:19:03,500
Something for alchemists
and witch doctors and science fiction.

939
01:19:07,166 --> 01:19:08,374
Until now.

940
01:19:08,375 --> 01:19:11,000
- [thrusters rumbling]
- [alarm blaring]

941
01:19:12,583 --> 01:19:14,541
[rumbling continues]

942
01:19:19,458 --> 01:19:20,915
V!

943
01:19:20,916 --> 01:19:22,999
It's okay! It's okay.

944
01:19:23,000 --> 01:19:25,083
[alarm continues]

945
01:19:27,916 --> 01:19:30,415
[screaming]

946
01:19:30,416 --> 01:19:32,791
[villager speaking foreign language]

947
01:19:34,166 --> 01:19:35,958
[screaming]

948
01:19:40,666 --> 01:19:43,165
We've had a fully-sequenced
human genome for decades.

949
01:19:43,166 --> 01:19:44,958
We know the face of our villain.

950
01:19:45,666 --> 01:19:48,791
This strand of DNA, CH14,

951
01:19:49,375 --> 01:19:50,665
it's responsible for turning off

952
01:19:50,666 --> 01:19:53,416
our ability to repair
and rebuild cellular structures.

953
01:19:54,166 --> 01:19:57,874
Early experiments with CRISPR
let us snip and stitch DNA in and out,

954
01:19:57,875 --> 01:20:02,875
which we thought would be enough,
but aging, decay, is built in.

955
01:20:03,666 --> 01:20:05,208
It's in more than one place.

956
01:20:06,750 --> 01:20:09,875
So, what we've done is created
a system of self-repair.

957
01:20:11,041 --> 01:20:13,125
Of maintenance at the molecular level

958
01:20:14,250 --> 01:20:17,750
that will mend what breaks,
repair what fails,

959
01:20:18,416 --> 01:20:21,374
and trick the body
into continuing to produce fresh,

960
01:20:21,375 --> 01:20:24,499
new, healthy cells far longer
than ever before.

961
01:20:24,500 --> 01:20:26,583
[audience applauding, cheering]

962
01:20:30,875 --> 01:20:33,208
- [ship powering down]
- It's okay. We're done. We're landing.

963
01:20:34,500 --> 01:20:36,375
V, I need your help.

964
01:20:38,833 --> 01:20:40,165
Everyone, stay put.

965
01:20:40,166 --> 01:20:42,250
[baby crying]

966
01:21:05,750 --> 01:21:08,333
- [sighs, sniffs]
- [crying continues]

967
01:21:10,416 --> 01:21:15,040
A gene replacement
technology that we call ELIXIR.

968
01:21:15,041 --> 01:21:17,125
[audience applauding, cheering]

969
01:21:24,541 --> 01:21:26,166
- [Coakley] Okay.
- [grunts]

970
01:21:26,916 --> 01:21:27,957
And you'll--

971
01:21:27,958 --> 01:21:30,290
I'll keep the kids safe until you're back.

972
01:21:30,291 --> 01:21:31,875
[baby crying]

973
01:21:43,541 --> 01:21:45,040
- [applauding continues]
- [sighs]

974
01:21:45,041 --> 01:21:46,500
That is our kid.

975
01:21:48,375 --> 01:21:49,375
We made him.

976
01:21:52,041 --> 01:21:53,041
How?

977
01:21:58,958 --> 01:22:03,415
[David] <i>Imagine if our great minds,
our Einsteins, our da Vincis,</i>

978
01:22:03,416 --> 01:22:07,750
<i>our Mozarts could live
200, 300, 500 years.</i>

979
01:22:09,583 --> 01:22:12,665
<i>The human lifespan has always been,
to the universe around us,</i>

980
01:22:12,666 --> 01:22:15,625
<i>nothing more than the blink of an eye.</i>

981
01:22:17,500 --> 01:22:20,375
<i>That's about to change.</i>

982
01:22:21,625 --> 01:22:25,833
For all of history,
time has been the enemy.

983
01:22:26,958 --> 01:22:30,540
Imagine what we'll accomplish
now that we've beaten it.

984
01:22:30,541 --> 01:22:32,750
[audience applauding]

985
01:22:50,416 --> 01:22:52,375
[Coakley] No. No, it's-- it's for you.

986
01:22:53,958 --> 01:22:54,958
You're the future.

987
01:23:59,208 --> 01:24:00,500
Would you wanna do it?

988
01:24:03,000 --> 01:24:04,000
Live forever?

989
01:24:06,291 --> 01:24:07,291
No.

990
01:24:16,916 --> 01:24:17,958
[speaking foreign language]

991
01:24:19,583 --> 01:24:21,000
[Claire] <i>I don't know.</i>

992
01:24:21,791 --> 01:24:25,791
<i>Life seems so improbable.</i>

993
01:24:26,791 --> 01:24:28,208
[speaking foreign language]

994
01:24:30,166 --> 01:24:31,958
<i>Finding each other.</i>

995
01:24:33,666 --> 01:24:34,749
<i>Building a life.</i>

996
01:24:34,750 --> 01:24:36,041
[speaking foreign language]

997
01:24:37,375 --> 01:24:39,208
<i>Making little people.</i>

998
01:24:39,875 --> 01:24:41,250
[speaking foreign language]

999
01:24:43,333 --> 01:24:44,958
[speaking foreign language]

1000
01:24:46,416 --> 01:24:48,041
[Claire] <i>Watching them grow.</i>

1001
01:24:50,875 --> 01:24:55,999
<i>And then, suddenly,
it-it all makes sense looking back.</i>

1002
01:24:56,000 --> 01:24:57,083
[David] Hey, Mom.

1003
01:25:00,666 --> 01:25:01,750
Hey.

1004
01:25:02,291 --> 01:25:03,291
Greg?

1005
01:25:04,416 --> 01:25:06,791
No, no, it's Dave.

1006
01:25:07,541 --> 01:25:09,375
No. [chuckles]

1007
01:25:13,958 --> 01:25:16,291
[Claire] <i>But that's
because you know it will end.</i>

1008
01:25:18,208 --> 01:25:21,041
<i>That's-- That's the point.
That's where the meaning is.</i>

1009
01:25:22,666 --> 01:25:23,916
<i>It ends.</i>

1010
01:25:25,125 --> 01:25:27,541
[instrument playing]

1011
01:25:40,666 --> 01:25:42,208
[sibling sniffles, cries]

1012
01:25:47,000 --> 01:25:49,416
[clock ticking]

1013
01:26:21,291 --> 01:26:23,291
[no audible dialogue]

1014
01:26:25,666 --> 01:26:30,416
You weren't related to me any more
than any of the rest of you are, but,

1015
01:26:32,000 --> 01:26:34,875
uh, you're still my kid.

1016
01:26:37,875 --> 01:26:40,750
<i>I really didn't think
I'd live to see this all happen.</i>

1017
01:26:43,458 --> 01:26:45,666
<i>I thought I'd be
the last person ever, maybe.</i>

1018
01:26:51,791 --> 01:26:52,916
<i>I didn't think,</i>

1019
01:26:54,458 --> 01:26:55,708
<i>"What if we make it?</i>

1020
01:26:57,583 --> 01:26:59,000
<i>What if we survive?"</i>

1021
01:27:01,333 --> 01:27:03,208
<i>I did not think about time...</i>

1022
01:27:05,166 --> 01:27:06,041
<i>at all.</i>

1023
01:27:13,666 --> 01:27:16,583
{\an8}<i>I just couldn't imagine seeing you grow.</i>

1024
01:27:20,583 --> 01:27:24,291
<i>And I couldn't imagine,
once you were gone...</i>

1025
01:27:25,333 --> 01:27:26,875
<i>that I'd still see you.</i>

1026
01:27:35,125 --> 01:27:37,665
<i>And I'd see everyone
that was there before you.</i>

1027
01:27:37,666 --> 01:27:40,583
[no audible dialogue]

1028
01:28:07,875 --> 01:28:12,250
<i>And now I see you in your children...</i>

1029
01:28:14,500 --> 01:28:16,208
<i>and in your children's children.</i>

1030
01:28:22,125 --> 01:28:24,166
<i>I guess we never really go away.</i>

1031
01:28:29,125 --> 01:28:30,708
<i>And what a beautiful thing.</i>

1032
01:28:34,833 --> 01:28:36,416
<i>We're all still here.</i>